        <main class="content">
            <h1>Flint Documentation</h1>
            <p>Welcome to the official documentation for the Flint programming language.</p>

            <h2 id="variables">Variables & Types</h2>
            <p>Flint supports dynamic typing including numbers, strings, booleans (<code>true</code>/<code>false</code>), and <code>nothing</code> (null). Declare variables using <code>let</code>.</p>
            <pre>let name = "Ignite";
let version = 1.0;
let isActive = true;
let emptyState = nothing;

// Multiple declarations are supported
let a = 1, b = 2, c;</pre>

            <h2 id="control-flow">Control Flow</h2>
            <p>Flint supports standard <code>if/else</code> logic, ternary operators, <code>while</code> loops, and <code>for</code> loops.</p>
            <pre>if (version >= 1.0) {
    print("Stable");
} else {
    print("Beta");
}

let parity = (a % 2 == 0) ? "Even" : "Odd";

for (let i = 0; i < 5; i = i + 1) {
    if (i == 3) continue;
    print(i);
}</pre>

            <h2 id="functions">Functions & Lambdas</h2>
            <p>Define named functions using the <code>func</code> keyword. Flint also supports first-class anonymous functions (lambdas) and closures.</p>
            <pre>func add(x, y) {
    return x + y;
}

// Anonymous lambda assignment
let multiply = func(a, b) { return a * b; };</pre>

            <h2 id="classes">Classes & Object-Oriented Programming</h2>
            <p>Flint features single-inheritance classes. Use <code>init()</code> for constructors, <code>class</code> for static methods, and <code>super</code> to access parent methods.</p>
            <pre>class Animal {
    class species() { return "Organic"; }
    speak() { print("Sound!"); }
}

class Dog < Animal {
    init(name) {
        this.name = name;
    }
    speak() {
        super.speak();
        print(" Bark!");
    }
}</pre>

            <h2 id="arrays-strings">Arrays & Strings</h2>
            <p>Flint includes native array data structures and string manipulation methods.</p>
            <pre>let arr = [10, 20, 30];
arr.push(40);
let last = arr.pop();
print(arr.length()); // Outputs 3
arr[0] = 99;

let text = "flint";
text.upper(); // "FLINT"
text.lower(); // "flint"</pre>

            <h2 id="builtins">Built-in Native Functions</h2>
            <p>Flint interfaces natively with C++ via these globally available functions:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><code>print(...)</code> - Variadic output to console.</li>
                <li><code>scan(prompt)</code> - Reads stdin from the user. Automatically parses numbers.</li>
                <li><code>clock()</code> - Returns system time in seconds.</li>
                <li><code>intDiv(a, b)</code> - Performs floor division.</li>
                <li><code>ord(str)</code> / <code>chr(num)</code> - Converts between characters and ASCII codes.</li>
                <li><code>toString(num)</code> - Coerces numbers to strings.</li>
            </ul>
        </main>
